  "raw_text": "Step 1: Start by isolating the square root term. Subtract 4 from both sides of the equation to get:\n\\[ x - 4 = \\sqrt{11 - 2x} \\]\n\nStep 2: Square both sides to eliminate the square root:\n\\[ (x - 4)^2 = 11 - 2x \\]\n\nStep 3: Expand the left side:\n\\[ x^2 - 8x + 16 = 11 - 2x \\]\n\nStep 4: Move all terms to one side to form a quadratic equation:\n\\[ x^2 - 8x + 16 - 11 + 2x = 0 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 6x + 5 = 0 \\]\n\nStep 5: Factor the quadratic equation:\n\\[ (x - 5)(x - 1) = 0 \\]\n\nStep 6: Solve for \\(x\\) by setting each factor equal to zero:\n\\[ x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 1 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 1 \\]\n\nStep 7: Check the solutions in the original equation to ensure they are valid. Substitute \\(x = 5\\):\n\\[ 5 = \\sqrt{11 - 2(5)} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 = \\sqrt{1} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 = 1 + 4 \\]\n\\[ 5 = 5 \\]\nThis is true, so \\(x = 5\\) is a valid solution.\n\nNow substitute \\(x = 1\\):\n\\[ 1 = \\sqrt{11 - 2(1)} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 1 = \\sqrt{9} + 4 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 3 + 4 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 7 \\]\nThis is false, so \\(x = 1\\) is not a valid solution.\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{5}<|im_end|>",
